== The Mystery and Practice of Marriage

=== §. 90. #emph[Of the mystery of the union of Christ and the Church.]

#align(center)[
  #block(width: 85%, breakable: false)[
    #set par(justify: false)
    #text(size: 0.9em, weight: 600)[Eph 5:32.]

    #text(style: "italic")[This is a great mystery, but I am speaking about Christ and the church.]
  ]
]

#v(0.8em)

#smallcaps[This] verse is the conclusion of that excellent digression which the apostle has made concerning the close union of Christ and the church.

Two points are to be noted in it.

1\. A moving exclamation (“This is a great mystery”).

2\. A particular application of the law named before (“but I am speaking about Christ and the church”).

Here first note that

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[#emph[The union between Christ and the church is a great mystery.]]

The apostle could not have said more of it than to call it #emph[a mystery, a great mystery.]

A #emph[mystery] is a divine secret. #footnote[On a mystery, see more in The Whole Armor of God, Treatise 3, §. 168, on Eph 6:19.]

It is a #emph[secret] in two respects.

1\. Because it is not known.

2\. Because it is unsearchable: its depth cannot be fathomed.

It is a #emph[divine] secret, for two other reasons.

1\. Because it could not have been opened except by divine revelation.

2\. Because, when it is opened, it cannot be grasped except by the illumination of the Spirit. The Spirit who opens and reveals the mystery must also open the eyes of our understanding to discern it rightly.

It is further said to be a #emph[great] mystery.

1\. #emph[Simply,] in itself, because its matter is deep, #footnote[1\. #emph[Use.] The mystery of our union with Christ is not to be measured by the line of our reason.] difficult, weighty, and of great importance.

2\. #emph[Comparatively,] in relation to other mysteries: no mystery revealed in God’s word is comparable to it.

Let us not presume to measure it with the line of our own reason. Since it is #emph[a great mystery,] it is above our capacity. Yet because it is revealed, we must believe it, as we believe the mysteries of the Trinity, of Christ’s eternal generation, of the personal union of his two natures, of the procession of the Holy Spirit, and the like. We believe them because the word has revealed them, though we cannot fully see the reason of them. Here lies a chief difference between our condition in this world and in the world to come. Here we must believe what we know only in part; there we shall perfectly know whatever is to be believed. Preachers can make this mystery known only in part, and hearers can grasp it only in part. Let us therefore wait for a perfect understanding of it until all things are perfected in Christ. But in the meantime let us believe, without doubting or wavering, what is revealed of it.

In our meditation on this mystery, let us conceive nothing carnal, #footnote[2\. #emph[Use.] Nothing carnal in our union with Christ. #emph[Ecclesia Christo in occulto vxor est. Occultè quippe atque intus in abscondito secreto spiritali anima humana inhaeret verbo Dei, vt sint duo in carne vna.] \[The church is Christ’s wife in secret. For secretly and inwardly, in a hidden, spiritual secret, the human soul cleaves to the Word of God, so that they are two in one flesh.\] #emph[Aug. contr. Faust. Man. lib.] 22. #emph[cap.] 38.] nothing earthly of it, because it is a mystery: it is altogether spiritual and heavenly. From the natural union of our head and body, and from the marriage union of man and wife, we may and ought to take occasion, by way of likeness, to help our understanding of the union of Christ and his church. It is for this end that these likenesses are used, and by this means our understanding may be much helped, as it is by the outward elements and rites used in the sacraments. But if, because of these comparisons, we drag this, which is only and wholly spiritual, into any carnal matter, we shall make a thick mist and a dark cloud of what is given for a light.

The folly of our adversaries is plainly exposed here. They make our union with Christ purely carnal. For they imagine it to consist in a bodily mingling of Christ’s flesh with ours: by our eating his flesh with the teeth of our bodies, drinking his blood down our throats, and digesting both #footnote[3\. #emph[Use.] Papists make our union with Christ a carnal matter. John 6:52. John 3:4.] in our stomachs as our bodily food, so that it may turn into our substance. In this they show themselves like the dull-headed people of Capernaum, and like ignorant Nicodemus. There is a great deal of gross absurdity in that notion, but no great mystery.

=== §. 91. #emph[Of the Popes usurping to be Spouse of the Church.]

The apostle’s application of this mystery to Christ and the church exposes two gross errors of the papists.

One is that they make the pope a spouse of the church. How can anyone have the face to apply to the pope and the church what the apostle says so expressly is meant of Christ and the #footnote[#emph[Multos adulteros video qui sponsam tanto pretio emptam possidere volunt, & id agunt vt pro sponso amentur.] \[I see many adulterers who want to possess the bride bought at so great a price, and who work to be loved in place of the bridegroom.\] #emph[Aug. in Ioh. Tract.] 12. #emph[Vide Bern. ep.] 237. #emph[ad Eugen.]] church? Indeed, what arrogant presumption it is to attribute to a mortal, sinful man what belongs to the eternal and holy Son of God alone! Is not this to confer Christ’s prerogatives on himself, and so make himself an outright Antichrist? Who gave the church to the pope, or the pope to the church? When did she give her consent? (I speak of the true catholic church of Christ, the universal church.) What has he done for her? Or rather, what has he not done against her? The #footnote[See §. 17.] distinction between an #emph[imperial] and a #emph[ministerial] spouse cannot serve their turn here. The metaphor of a head will not allow a ministerial head, and much less will the metaphor of a spouse allow a ministerial spouse. As he is an adulterer who takes it on himself to be a ministerial husband, so she is an adulteress who yields herself to such a one. The apostle says, “I promised you#super[+] to ONE husband.” #footnote[2 Cor 11:2.]

=== §. 92. #emph[Of the false Sacrament of Marriage.]

The other error is that #emph[marriage is a sacrament.] The main ground of it they have taken from this text. Yet that ground is removed by the apostle’s application of this mystery to Christ and the church as plainly as if the apostle had framed his words on purpose to forestall this wrong inference. It is as if he had said, #emph[So that no one may mistake this mystery and apply it to the joining of man and woman in marriage, know that I mean no such thing: the mystery I speak of concerns Christ and the church.] I wonder how they dare misapply what is expressed so plainly. Even if the apostle had not shown his mind and meaning so clearly, the very thing itself would lead us to judge so of it. For “that which in Christ #footnote[#emph[Quod est in Christo & in Ecclesia magnum, hoc in singulis quibusque viris & uxoribus minimum. Aug. de nupt. & concup. lib.] 1. #emph[cap.] 21.] and the church is a great mystery is only a small matter in man and wife.” The Latin Vulgate first led them into this error, for it translates the word #emph[mystery] as #emph[a sacrament.] But a translation is no sufficient ground to prove a doctrine. Besides, the word #emph[sacrament] used by that translator has as wide a range as #emph[mystery.] If they were to make everything he translates #emph[sacrament] a proper sacrament of the church, there would be many more sacraments than the papists themselves make.

1\. As for this supposed sacrament, no papist could ever show when or where God ordained it to be a sacrament. Indeed, they do not agree among themselves about the time, how long it has been a sacrament. #footnote[#emph[Alphons. à Castro contr. haer. lib.] 3.] Some of them hold that it has been a sacrament ever since the first institution of marriage in Paradise. But the greater number of papists hold it to be a sacrament #footnote[#emph[Petrus à Soto de Matr. lect.] 2. #emph[Concil. Trident. Sess.] 24. #emph[Can.] 1.] of the New Testament, under the gospel, because their Council of Trent has so decreed. Here we may note how the greater number of them, when two absurdities are in question, are ready to fall into the worse. Under the law the church in its childhood needed, and had, more sacraments than under the gospel. Yet this thing, which was in use as much under the law as under the gospel, and had as much then to make it a sacrament as it has now, was no sacrament then, and yet is one now.

2\. As they cannot show where it was ordained as a sacrament, neither can they show what its sacramental sign is. Some make the bodily union of man and wife the sign. But there may be a true marriage though the parties married never come together.

Others make #emph[the parents’ giving] the sign. But they hold that a marriage made without the parents’ consent is a true marriage.

Others make it #emph[the priest’s blessing.] Yet they hold that the marriage of infidels and heretics, who have no priests, is a true marriage.

Others make it #emph[the consent of the parties themselves.] Then a party would administer a sacrament to himself.

Others make it other things. So, lacking the light of God’s word, one strays down one bypath and another down another, and none of them hits on the right one.

3\. There is a similar disagreement about the form of this sacrament.

4\. If the other positions they lay down concerning marriage are noted, one would think they would be far from making it a sacrament. They prefer virginity to it. Indeed, they count it a kind of pollution. They hold it unlawful for priests, monks, nuns, and such holy orders (as they esteem them) to marry. So there is a sacrament in which their holy ones may not share. The order of priesthood is a sacrament (in their account), yet that order keeps men from marriage; so one sacrament fights against another. Indeed, infidels may partake of a sacrament, and so their holy and precious things are denied to their holy ones and cast to swine. So we see a rotten building raised on a sandy foundation: a false sacrament established on a false application of this text. Can it then stand?

=== §. 93. #emph[Of the Sum of husbands and wives duties.]

#align(center)[
  #block(width: 85%, breakable: false)[
    #set par(justify: false)
    #text(size: 0.9em, weight: 600)[Eph 5:33.]

    #text(style: "italic")[Nevertheless, let each one of you#super[+] in particular so love his wife as himself, and let the wife see that she reverences her husband.]
  ]
]

#v(0.8em)

#smallcaps[The] apostle, having made a long digression about the mutual relation between Christ and the church, whom he set forth as patterns to husbands and wives, now returns to the main point he intended, namely the duties of husbands and wives. The first word #emph[(Nevertheless)] implies as much, as if he had said, #emph[Though I have digressed a little] #footnote[πλήν.] #emph[into the mystery of the union of Christ and the church, yet nevertheless, O husbands and wives, call to mind what I chiefly aimed at, namely your#super[+] duties.]

This verse, then, contains the conclusion of the apostle’s discourse concerning the duties of husbands and wives.

Two points are especially to be noted in it.

+ A declaration of their separate and distinct duties.
+ A direction to each of them to apply their own proper duties to themselves.

Their distinct duties are noted in two words

- Love.
- Fear.

These two, as they are distinct duties in themselves, so they are also common conditions that must be joined to all other duties. #emph[Love] is as sugar to sweeten the duties of authority that belong to a husband. #emph[Fear] is as salt to season all the duties of subjection that belong to a wife. The apostle has therefore set them down as two marks for husbands and wives to aim at in everything in which they have to deal with one another.

I will speak of these more distinctly in the treatises on the particular duties of husbands and wives.

=== §. 94. #emph[Of applying the word to our selves.]

The direction for each of them to apply his own proper duties to himself in particular is especially to be noted here. Two things are to be observed in this direction.

1\. That every particular person apply to himself what a minister delivers to all in general. “Each one #footnote[ὑμεῖς οἱ καθ’ ἕνα.] of you#super[+] in particular,” says the apostle. This is as much as if he had expressed his mind more fully in this way: #emph[I have laid down such general duties as all husbands and wives, without exception, of whatever rank or degree they are, are bound to. I have not delivered them by name to every one separately, one by one, but generally to you#super[+] all; yet let every one of you apply these things to yourselves in particular.]

2\. That every one apply his own special duty to himself. Since love belongs especially to a husband, to him he says that he must “love his wife.” And since reverence belongs especially to a wife, to her he says, “let the wife see that she reverences her husband.”

There is a direction in each of the separate letters sent to the seven churches of Asia, in these words: “He who #footnote[Rev 2:7.] has an ear, let him hear what the Spirit says to the churches.” It teaches every member of any of those churches to apply to himself what was delivered to the whole church. So does a similar #footnote[Luke 8:8.] exhortation which Christ called out to the people whom he taught in parables. So does this declaration of how far Christ’s counsel reaches: “What I say to #footnote[Mark 13:37.] you#super[+], I say to everyone.” To this purpose many precepts given to whole churches, and to all sorts of people, are set down in the singular number, as if given to one: “Wake up, YOU \[singular\] who #footnote[Eph 5:14.] sleep.” “YOU \[singular\] stand by faith. Do not be arrogant,” etc. #footnote[Rom 11:20.]

The life and power of God’s word lies in this particular #footnote[#emph[Reason.] The life of God’s word lies in application.] application of it to ourselves. This is to mix #emph[faith with hearing.] I mean a faith by which we not only believe the truth of God’s word in general, but also believe it to be a truth concerning ourselves in particular. In this way every precept in it will be a good instruction and direction to guide us in the way of righteousness. Every promise in it will be a great encouragement and consolation to us, to uphold us and make us hold on. And every judgment threatened in it will be a curb and bridle to hold us in, and to keep us from the sins against which the judgments are threatened. But otherwise, if we do not bring the word home to our own souls, it will be as a word “spoken into the air,” #footnote[1 Cor 14:9.] vanishing away without any profit to us. Nothing makes the word less profitable than putting it off from ourselves onto others, thinking that it concerns others more than ourselves.

That we may make the better use of this doctrine, let us observe both what general duties belong to all Christians, and apply them to ourselves as particular; and also what duties belong to persons of our own place, calling and condition, and apply them to ourselves more especially. Let all manner of husbands and all manner of wives, of whatever rank or degree they are, who read the duties that follow, know that they are spoken to them in particular. Let kings and queens, lords and ladies, ministers and their wives, rich men and their wives, poor men and their wives, old men and their wives, young men and their wives, all of every sort, take them as spoken to them in particular. It is not honor, wealth, learning, or any other excellence, nor lowliness of place, poverty, lack of learning, or any other such thing, that can exempt a husband from loving his wife, or a wife from reverencing her husband. He who says #emph[each one] excepts #emph[no one.] Therefore, #emph[each one in particular,] do you#super[+] so. The same application may be made to all parents and children, masters and servants, concerning their duties.

=== §. 95. #emph[Of every ones looking to his own duty especially.]

In this application we must look rather to the duty which we owe and ought to perform to others than to what is due to us and others ought to perform to us. For the apostle does not say to the husband, “See that your wife reverences you,” but, “See that you love her”; and so to the wife.

For this purpose the Holy Spirit presses particular duties on the particular persons who ought to perform them: #emph[subjection] on #emph[wives,] #emph[love] on #emph[husbands,] and so with others. So this is what you must especially consider: how you #footnote[#emph[Vnum duntaxat tibi considerandum est, quo pacto te praestes innoxium. Chrys. hom.] 26. #emph[in] 1 Cor 11.] may show yourself blameless. I do not deny that one ought to stir up another, and one help another as far as they can to perform their duty, especially superiors who have charge over others. But the chief care of every one ought to be for himself, and he ought to make the greatest conscience of performing his own duty.

1\. It is more acceptable before God, and more commendable #footnote[Reasons.] before men, to do duty than to exact duty. As in matters of free charity, so also in matters of bound duty, “It is more blessed to #footnote[Acts 20:35.] give than to receive.” In particular, it is better for a husband to be a good husband than to have a good wife; and so for a wife. To have others fail in their duty to us may be a heavy cross; for us to fail in our duty to others is a fearful curse.

2\. Every one is to give an account of his own particular #footnote[Rom 14:12.] duty. What the prophet says of father and son may be applied to husband and wife, and to all other sorts of people: “If a father does what is just and right, he is righteous; he #footnote[Ezek 18:5. #emph[etc.]] will surely live. If he has a son who does not do so, he will surely die; his blood will be on his own head.” Again: “If a father does what is not good, he will die in his iniquity; but if his son does what is just and right, he will surely live. The righteousness of the righteous man will fall upon him, and the wickedness of the wicked man will fall upon him.” That it will be so between husband and wife may be gathered from these words: “Two people #footnote[Luke 17:34.] will be in one bed: one will be taken and the other left.”

Let this be noted against the common, empty excuse made for neglecting duty, which is this: #emph[Duty is not performed to me; why should I do my duty?] “When my husband does his duty, I will do mine,” says the wife. “And I mine,” says the husband, “when my wife does hers.” What if he never does his duty, and so is damned? Will you never do yours? This looking for duty at others’ hands makes us all the more careless of our own.

Therefore, you#super[+] husbands, look especially to your #footnote[Use.] own duties: love your wives. And you#super[+] wives, look #footnote[Non quae alijs data sunt praecepta quaerimus quando alicuius criminis accusamur. \[When we are accused of some fault, we do not look for the precepts given to others.\] Chrys. loc. citat.] especially to yours: reverence your husbands. For this end, let husbands read most diligently the duties which concern husbands, and wives those which concern wives. Let not the husband say of the wife’s duties, “There are good lessons for my wife,” and neglect his own; nor the wife say the same of the husband’s duties, and not regard her own. This is what makes the subjection of many wives very harsh and irksome to them: their husbands, who urge and press them to it, show little or no love to them at all. And this is what makes many husbands very slow to show love: their wives, who expect much love, show little or no reverence to their husbands. Therefore, “let each one of you#super[+] in particular so love his wife as himself, and let the wife see that she reverences her husband.”
